Cisco Router & Switch Configuration Backup Automation
Automated backup of Cisco router and switch configurations using Python, Netmiko, and Linux cron. The system reads device inventory from an Excel spreadsheet, connects to devices over SSH through the management subnet, stores timestamped configuration backups, maintains version history, and detects configuration changes using file diffs.

Features
Excel inventory: Reads hostname, management IP, username, password, device type, and optional enable secret.
SSH connectivity: Uses Netmiko to connect to Cisco routers and switches.
Automated backups: Collects the running configuration using show running-config.
Timestamped version history: Saves a new configuration file on every successful run without overwriting previous backups.
Configuration change detection: Compares the latest configuration with the previous successful backup.
Diff generation: Creates unified diff files when configuration changes are detected.
Email alerts: Optionally sends email notifications with configuration diffs attached.
Error handling: Continues processing other devices when a device connection or backup fails.
Execution logging: Records backup results, configuration changes, and failures.
Cron scheduling: Supports scheduled backups using Linux cron.
Architecture

                    Linux Backup Server
                   +---------------------+
                   | Python Backup Script|
                   | Netmiko + openpyxl  |
                   | Diff Detection      |
                   | Logging / Alerts    |
                   +----------+----------+
                              |
                      Management Subnet
                              |
               +--------------+--------------+
               |                             |
        +------v------+               +------v------+
        | Cisco Router|               | Cisco Switch|
        | Management IP              | Management IP|
        +-------------+               +-------------+
                              |
                              v
                    Local Backup Repository
                    /var/backups/cisco/
Technology Stack

Component	Purpose
Python 3	Backup automation
Netmiko	SSH connectivity and Cisco CLI interaction
openpyxl	Reading Excel inventory
difflib	Configuration comparison and diff generation
smtplib	Optional email notifications
Linux cron	Scheduled execution
Project Structure

cisco-config-backup/
├── cisco_backup.py
├── devices.xlsx
├── README.md
├── requirements.txt
└── logs/
    ├── backup.log
    └── cron.log

/var/backups/cisco/
├── CORE-SW-01/
│   ├── <timestamp>_running-config.txt
│   ├── <timestamp>_running-config.txt
│   └── <timestamp>_changes.diff
└── EDGE-RTR-01/
    ├── <timestamp>_running-config.txt
    └── <timestamp>_changes.diff
The backup repository is separate from the Git project directory.

Prerequisites
Linux server with Python 3 installed.
SSH reachability from the Linux server to the device management IP addresses.
Cisco routers and switches with CLI access enabled.
A dedicated device account authorized to execute show running-config.
An Excel inventory file containing device credentials.
Network access to an SMTP server if email alerts are required.
Installation
1. Clone the repository

git clone <YOUR_GIT_REPOSITORY_URL>
cd cisco-config-backup
Replace the placeholder with your actual Git repository URL.

2. Create a Python virtual environment

python3 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate
3. Install dependencies
Create a requirements.txt file:


netmiko
openpyxl
Install the dependencies:


pip install -r requirements.txt
For reproducible deployments, pin and maintain tested dependency versions.

4. Create directories

sudo mkdir -p /var/backups/cisco
mkdir -p logs
Ensure the account running the script has appropriate permissions to write to the backup and log directories.

Excel Inventory Configuration
Create devices.xlsx with the following column headers in the first worksheet:


hostname	ip	username	password	device_type	secret
CORE-SW-01	10.10.10.11	backupuser	CHANGE_ME	cisco_ios	
EDGE-RTR-01	10.10.10.21	backupuser	CHANGE_ME	cisco_ios	
Column descriptions

Column	Required	Description
hostname	Yes	Device name used for backup folders
ip	Yes	Device management IP address
username	Yes	SSH username
password	Yes	SSH password
device_type	No	Netmiko platform identifier; defaults to cisco_ios
secret	No	Enable password, if required
Common Netmiko platform identifiers include cisco_ios and cisco_nxos. Confirm the appropriate identifier for your device and software platform.

Security: Do not commit real device passwords, enable secrets, or other credentials to Git. For production use, retrieve credentials from a protected secrets store or an appropriately secured credential file.

Configure the Backup Script
Update the configuration variables at the beginning of cisco_backup.py if your paths differ:


BASE_DIR = Path("/opt/cisco-backup")
EXCEL_FILE = BASE_DIR / "devices.xlsx"
BACKUP_ROOT = Path("/var/backups/cisco")
LOG_DIR = BASE_DIR / "logs"
Make sure the script's actual location matches BASE_DIR.

The script retrieves the running configuration using:


show running-config
Each device's configuration is stored in a separate directory. Every successful backup receives a unique timestamped filename.

Run the Backup Manually
Activate the virtual environment and execute the script:


source venv/bin/activate
python cisco_backup.py
Or execute it using the virtual environment's Python interpreter:


/opt/cisco-backup/venv/bin/python \
  /opt/cisco-backup/cisco_backup.py
Check the exit status:


echo $?
Expected exit codes:

0: All device backups succeeded.
1: One or more device backups failed.
2: The inventory could not be loaded or contained no valid devices.
Backup Storage and Version History
Backups are stored under:


/var/backups/cisco/<hostname>/
Example filenames:


2026-10-20_120000_000000_+0530_running-config.txt
2026-10-20_180000_000000_+0530_running-config.txt
2026-10-20_180000_000000_+0530_changes.diff
Timestamps use the server's local timezone, including its UTC offset.

The script saves a new configuration file on every successful run. When a previous successful configuration exists, it compares the new configuration with that earlier version.

If the configuration changed, a unified diff file is generated.
If no changes are detected, the configuration is still saved, but no change diff is generated.
The first backup establishes the baseline and has no earlier version to compare against.
Configuration Change Alerts
The script can optionally send email notifications through an SMTP server.

Configure the following environment variables in a protected environment file or wrapper script:


export BACKUP_SMTP_HOST="smtp.example.com"
export BACKUP_SMTP_PORT="587"
export BACKUP_SMTP_USER="backup-alerts@example.com"
export BACKUP_SMTP_PASSWORD="SMTP_PASSWORD"
export BACKUP_ALERT_FROM="backup-alerts@example.com"
export BACKUP_ALERT_TO="network-team@example.com"
Ensure the scheduled execution environment loads these variables. Cron does not automatically inherit environment variables from an interactive shell.

When a configuration change is detected, the script logs the event and attempts to send an email with the diff attached. If SMTP is not configured, the change is still recorded locally.

Schedule Automated Backups with Cron
Edit the crontab for the account that runs the backup:


crontab -e
Example: schedule a backup every day at 6:00 PM.


0 18 * * * /opt/cisco-backup/venv/bin/python /opt/cisco-backup/cisco_backup.py >> /opt/cisco-backup/logs/cron.log 2>&1
Other examples:


Schedule	Cron expression
Daily at midnight	0 0 * * *
Daily at 6:00 AM	0 6 * * *
Daily at 6:00 PM	0 18 * * *
Every six hours	0 */6 * * *
Every Sunday at 2:00 AM	0 2 * * 0
Cron uses the server's configured timezone. Check it using:


timedatectl
Verify the scheduled job using:


crontab -l
Logging and Troubleshooting
The application log is stored at:


/opt/cisco-backup/logs/backup.log
Cron output is stored at:


/opt/cisco-backup/logs/cron.log
Follow the application log:


tail -f /opt/cisco-backup/logs/backup.log
Common troubleshooting steps:

Verify management subnet connectivity and routing.
Verify SSH access to the device management IP.
Check device credentials and enable privileges.
Confirm the Netmiko device_type matches the target platform.
Verify the Excel column headers and required values.
Confirm write permissions on the backup and log directories.
Check SMTP settings and network access if email alerts fail.
Verify the cron environment uses the correct Python interpreter and configuration paths.
Security Considerations
Use a dedicated least-privilege device account.
Restrict access to the Excel inventory and any credential files.
Do not commit passwords, SMTP credentials, or sensitive configurations to Git.
Use SSH host-key verification or another approved server-identity validation mechanism for production deployments.
Restrict access to the backup repository because device configurations may contain sensitive information.
Encrypt backup storage and secure off-server copies.
Define and implement a backup retention policy.
Test recovery and configuration comparison procedures regularly.
Limitations
The script backs up the running configuration; it does not automatically save the Cisco startup configuration.
It requires management network reachability and working SSH credentials.
Email alerts require a correctly configured SMTP service.
Configuration diffs are text-based and may include changes caused by ordering or device-generated output.
Backup retention and archival policies must be configured separately.
Production deployments should test platform compatibility, command output, and privilege requirements.
Future Enhancements
Support for Cisco IOS-XE, NX-OS, and additional network platforms.
Encrypted credential management.
Configurable retention and automatic archival.
Failure notification and summary reports.
Centralized monitoring dashboard.
Backup integrity checks and encrypted off-site replication.
Support for additional commands, such as show startup-config, where required.
